## Rationale For Each Change

### Version and date

This identifies the document as an amendment rather than silently rewriting
the v0.3 baseline. The threshold was deferred by v0.3 until the real report
was available; Sprint 1 supplied that report on November 5.

### Test discovery requirement

The named incident is the **Project Structure CI Discovery Incident** (GitHub
Actions run for `Initial Frontend Development`, October 29, 2026). CI ran the
v0.3 test command, collected **0 items**, and failed with exit code 5. v0.3
said that pytest failures block merging, but it did not say that zero
collected tests is itself a failure or require a discovery configuration. That
was the wrong answer because the gate could not distinguish “the suite passed”
from “the suite never ran.” The amendment makes that distinction explicit.

### Pre-PR lint and formatting check

The named incident is the **Health Check, Timestamp, Service Status Ruff
Incident** (GitHub Actions run for that change, November 2, 2026). The PR
reached CI and `ruff check .` failed, requiring a follow-up lint repair. v0.3
listed linting and formatting as CI gates, but gave the author no required
local pre-PR check. That was the wrong answer because a cheap deterministic
check was deferred until after review and CI time had already been spent. The
amendment makes the same commands an author responsibility before review.

### 80% production-domain coverage floor

The report measured 85% across the named production monitoring modules (270
statements, 40 missed, 14 tests passing). The floor is intentionally 80%, not
85%: it is a five-point buffer against normal semester changes while still
requiring meaningful coverage of the domain layer. It is a numeric,
merge-blocking floor for the named production monitoring files, not a promise
about the whole repository. Tests and migrations are excluded so they cannot
inflate the commitment.